  "early_life": {
    "en": "Born in Königsberg, Prussia, Kirchhoff studied at the University of Königsberg under Franz Neumann. He showed exceptional talent in mathematical physics and began his academic career at the University of Berlin.",
    "es": "",
    "pt": "",
    "fr": "",
    "de": "",
    "zh": "",
    "hi": "",
    "ar": "",
    "id": "",
    "ja": ""
  },
  "impact": {
    "en": "Kirchhoff's circuit laws became foundational principles for electrical engineering. His work on spectroscopy with Robert Bunsen led to the discovery of cesium and rubidium and enabled the chemical analysis of distant stars, revolutionizing astronomy.",
